Hridi

I see you have followed Dr Howe's suggestion to use body size as a covariate in your multi-species detection function modelling. That's fine. Alternatively, you could subset your data by species and fit detection functions individually to the detections of each species; I presume you have sufficient detections for all species to support species-specific detection functions. The subsetting analysis avoids making the assumption that all species of interest share the same key function shape; that's not usually a strong assumption and I don't suspect results from the two approaches would be substantially different.

Now for the warnings the ds​ function is producing from your hazard rate, body-mass covariate with simple polynomial adjustment model. The warning literally says that the fitted model produces detection probabilities that increase as a function of distance over some range of distances. This is inconsistent with one of the distance sampling principles. Sometimes adjustment terms introduce "bumps" in the fitted detection function model leading to this behaviour.

The warning suggests you investigate your data and query whether this is appropriate. Is it plausible that detection probability increases in your camera field of view? It looks from the PDF figure you have provided that this "bump" occurs at small distances. That may be problematic for the estimation of h(0), the most important parameter in detection function modelling when analysing point transect data.

I cannot say more without becoming more acquainted with the data. Perhaps others have experience with the effect of non-monotonicity on CTDS estimates.
