Camille

As best I can make out, you're hybridising between the two data formats that  ds​ understands.

Either use the flatfile​ format to have all information in a single data frame, or create three data frames containing information about the strata, transects and detections. From what I can tell, I think you need a data frame for the argument obs_table​ and then dispense with the data​ argument.  Here is the story from the documentation for ds​

Data format

One can supply data only to simply fit a detection function. However, if abundance/density estimates are necessary further information is required. Either the region_table, sample_table and obs_table data.frames can be supplied or all data can be supplied as a "flat file" in the data argument. In this format each row in data has additional information that would ordinarily be in the other tables. This usually means that there are additional columns named: Sample.Label, Region.Label, Effort and Area for each observation. See MailScanner has detected a possible fraud attempt from "127.0.0.1:10779" claiming to be flatfile <http://127.0.0.1:10779/help/library/Distance/help/flatfile> for an example.
